# Two Lineages
Tamil title: இரு பரம்பரைகள்

“Are you sulking? Why are you angry, darling? Jimmy, you wicked fellow! Go and make peace with Julia.”

“Julie! Julie! Look here. Goodness! When our Julia grows angry with her husband, the commotion she creates cannot be described. See how she glares. Poor Jimmy pleaded and failed, then left, thinking Julia's anger might subside by itself.”

“Whatever happened, Julie, you should not become so angry.”

“When you too acquire a husband tomorrow...” Mari began teasing.

Deva twisted her ear and playfully rebuked her. “Be quiet! Must you always talk about marriage?”

Would Mari stop? She had seen many a “Deva” who once mocked talk of marriage later rock a cradle and kiss a sleeping child.

“Julia is very proud; are you not just as proud, Mother?” Mari continued.

Seeing Jimmy's pitiable, sorrowful gaze after failing to pacify Julia, Deva ran and brought pieces of biscuit. Could eating biscuits offer Jimmy the delight he found in flirting with Julia? He did not eat. Julia turned once to look at him, then contemptuously faced away again.

“Father, have you seen how beautiful those lapdogs are? Yesterday I touched and stroked Julia. It was like placing a hand upon a silk mattress.”

“They are beautiful dogs. They say Julia and Jimmy were bought in Bangalore. Do you know their price? For how much did we sell our millet field last month?”

“Two hundred and fifty.”

“The price of Julia and Jimmy was also two hundred and fifty.”

Sukhananda Mudaliar and his son Singaram sat in their tiled house, mortgaged to a Marwari, and spoke of Julia and Jimmy, the lapdogs holding court in Dhanapal Chettiar's bungalow.

Thus their house had been pledged and the millet field sold. The price of the land, ancestral family property for two generations, was the same as that of dogs who wagged even their bodies with refinement. There, two hundred and fifty rupees bought dogs; here it sold a field inherited for two generations.

What use was the name Sukhanandam in such a condition? He possessed neither *sukham*, comfort, nor *anandam*, joy.

Dhanapal Chettiar had once entered trade in partnership with Sukhanandam. Sukhanandam remained in the same modest business, while Dhanapal somehow became a millionaire. Yet he had not abandoned their old friendship. He invited Sukhanandam to every event at his house.

“Some fellows lose their heads when fortune comes. Our Dhanapal is not like that,” Sukhanandam told all his friends.

He habitually called him, “Why, Dhanapalu!” both to his face and while speaking to friends. Yet at times that same Sukhanandam was forced to call him “Chettiar.”

A watchman had been installed at the bungalow. Whoever wished to enter had first to answer: “Who are you? What business brings you?” Did the watchman know that Sukhanandam and Dhanapal had conducted the same trade and were friends? Even if he knew, what then? Dhanapal Chettiar was his master; Sukhanandam was merely some man.

One day he made Sukhanandam stand at the gate.

“Who are you?”

“Me? Who are you, fellow? Are you new? The arrangement itself seems new. I am his friend.”

After answering, Sukhanandam reflected angrily that he had been forced to say “he” and “him” of his own Dhanapalu.

“Whom do you wish to see?” the guard asked again.

Could he say “Dhanapalu”? Would that sound respectful? At once he answered, “I wish to see Dhanapal Chettiar.”

Then he thought: “I had to call our Dhanapalu 'Chettiar.' We shared one shop and one trade. Today he is rich, and I myself must call him Chettiar.” His anger increased.

“Wait here. I shall ask Master and return,” the servant said.

“Master, indeed! Why should I care? Shame! Coming to see him is itself a mistake. I should simply leave,” Sukhanandam thought. Then he decided that since he had come, he would this once see Dhanapalu, condemn the regime operating there, and depart.

“Dhanapalu! They say when a mean fellow prospers, he carries an umbrella at midnight. That is precisely the story here. What a mighty government operates now! Some fortune struck, and money accumulated. Does that mean all old respect, affection, and friendship must vanish? I imagined you alone would remain respectful instead of tossing your head like others. Once money came, you too began behaving like them. This is wrong. Wealth is not eternal. However much money one possesses, it is useless without character; only then will Lakshmi remain.”

Sukhanandam was prepared to pour out his heart in a torrent as he followed the servant. But Dhanapal Chettiar made all his anger fly away, not by speaking to Sukhanandam but by abusing the servant.

“You thick fellow! Did you call *him* some visitor? Fool! Who is he? Our friend! If anyone in this world cares about my family affairs, it is he. Blockhead! Whenever he comes, do not stop or question him. He is I, and I am he! Go! You cannot recognize a man's rank.”

Sukhanandam's very crown grew cool. He forgot himself. Dhanapal deserved to accumulate crores, he thought. How greatly he valued old friendship! What noble character! Money had gathered precisely because his character was such.

From that day, Sukhanandam himself began respectfully magnifying the friend who had magnified him. Such was their friendship.

Singaram's desire for the lapdogs repeatedly drew him to Dhanapal's bungalow. Would Jimmy and Julia attain liberation while still sulking? Julia gave birth to two beautiful pups and proudly enjoyed both them and Jimmy's delight. One pup was white, the other black. Their colours differed, but their beauty was equal.

Singaram was ready to perform penance to obtain either. He pleaded with his father.

“Father...”

“What?”

“That puppy...”

“Why do we need that affliction?”

“Go away, Father! It is so beautiful.”

“Yes, but it needs milk and biscuits worth eight annas every day.”

“Somehow we can manage. Ask Chettiar.”

“Dhanapalu will certainly give it if asked, but why do we need dog or cat?”

While father and son spoke thus, Dhanapal was placing the pups more profitably.

“Black? Shame, dirty! We do not want it,” an official said.

“There is a white puppy too, sir. One is for the Income Tax Officer, and I had already resolved to give you the other.”

“Very well, bring the white one. The peon already fails to do his work properly. Once this puppy arrives, that will finish it: he will become completely idle.”

Even the Deputy Collector complained to Dhanapal about the trouble the puppy would cause, while Chettiar took pride that a dog he gave would grow in the Collector's house.

When Singaram's pestering became unbearable and Sukhanandam asked Dhanapal for a puppy, he never imagined refusal. Would golden-hearted Dhanapalu deny a mere puppy to a childhood friend?

The answer stunned him.

“A puppy? Yes. The Deputy Collector begged and pleaded for this one. I explained, 'The Income Tax Officer wanted one, and I reluctantly agreed. Only one remains. How can my heart find peace if I give that away too?' But he is stubborn. 'Whether it costs a hundred or a thousand, I shall pay. You must give it,' he practically ordered. What can I do? Next time I shall certainly give you one.”

Sukhanandam was angry that his friend refused “a mere puppy.” But that “mere” animal was going to a Collector's house. What could he do? He polished the “next time” answer before giving it to his son.

Singaram was hurt but could say nothing after hearing “Deputy Collector” and “Income Tax Officer.” Deva appeared as his advocate.

“Father, what is this? Sukhanandam is so close to us. Why not give it to him? Who asked whether the Income Tax Officer wanted a dog? You yourself went and asked him.”

Dhanapal laughed at Deva's ignorance of the world.

“You fool! You mock me for going voluntarily. I myself went not only to the Income Tax Officer's house but to the Deputy Collector's and asked whether they wanted puppies. I regret there is no third. What do you know?”

“Do you mean that if there were another, you would give it to Sukhanandam? Poor man! At least you possess that much affection for your friend.”

“Foolish girl! I did not mean I would give it to that ill-fated face. I would give it to Multani, who lends us money when asked. Do you know how much we gain if one puppy from our house roams the Collector's bungalow and another the Income Tax Officer's? Whenever anyone asks, 'Where did the puppy come from?' they will say, 'Dhanapal Chettiar gave it.' How many times will my name be spoken? When senior officers mention me, how much respect will the town show?

“Great men will say Dhanapal Chettiar and the Collector are intimate friends, that even the puppy in the Collector's house came from Chettiar. These dogs will be our agents there. You ask me to give one to Sukhanandam without understanding. He will be angry for a while because he did not receive it. What loss is that? Compare it with the profit from having pups in those officers' homes. You are a merchant's daughter, yet possess no subtle intelligence.”

Deva gained clarity, not that her father's path was right, but that he was an apparatus for accumulating money. She learned that however inseparable a poor man and rich man might appear, even as friends from youth, their worlds, thoughts, arrangements, plans, and philosophies remained entirely different.

That night tears fell without her knowledge. The emotion was not causeless: love for Singaram had already been born and crawled within her. Singaram tried to bring into a debt-laden home a puppy destined for a Collector's bungalow. Dhanapal was preparing a mansion for his daughter, but she saw her beloved in the mud-walled house. What use was love flowering in the daughter of a man who sought profit even through a dog? It would wither, or he would crush and cast it away in anger.

“Next time” arrived, and Deva petitioned again for Singaram. This time her mother rejected it harshly.

“What a splendid idea! Those poor people have nothing. What happens if we give a dog to their house? Where will money come from for milk and biscuits? It will die in four days.”

True, but a truth that made the heart seethe. Deva wondered why wretched Julia had to become pregnant and produce another pup only for them to say it could not be given. How much Singaram would suffer!

Singaram himself expected to marry the girl next door, Annam, after the month of Margazhi. Yet he did not abandon his desire for a puppy and kept walking to Chettiar's house.

This time the local officer declined to accept it. Dhanapal therefore adorned his gift to Sukhanandam with words: “Can one forget an old friendship? Even if the Governor asked, I would not give it after promising you. A word is a word.”

Singaram gave George, as the puppy was named, more than half the kisses he had reserved for Annam.

Deva explained its care in detail: this much milk, so many biscuit pieces, a soft cloth at night. Could Singaram afford the milk, biscuits, and silk mattress? Deva did not consider it. In her heart he was “he,” her beloved, and therefore she imagined he could earn them. Love is a kind of madness.

That madness left Deva in one second and one sentence. After she finished her instructions, Singaram smiled and said, “Annam will take care of all that.” Deva understood that saying Annam's name had caused his smile.

“Who is Annam?” she asked.

“Do you not know? She is the girl he will marry when Thai begins,” Deva's mother answered.

Singaram stood smiling. Deva's madness cleared.

George cured Singaram of cinema, coffee, cards, and other habits. Every coin became biscuit, milk, or soap. Even the occasional firecracker flowers he lovingly bought for Annam became biscuits. He cared greatly that George should grow splendidly, regardless of expense.

But could this poor household provide the satisfaction suited to the dog? George grew thin, developed mange, cried endlessly, and became uglier every day.

Annam disliked Singaram wasting money on the puppy.

“What kind of stomach has it? It eats four annas of biscuits a day, yet only bones and skin remain.”

She tried to end the waste and suggested feeding George rice.

“Shame! For six months it must receive only milk and biscuits. Deva insisted upon it repeatedly, do you know?”

If Annam placed the puppy on the ground in the evening, he scolded her. The scolding itself hurt less than his frequent “Deva, Deva.” Hatred of Deva arose in Annam. One day she sarcastically suggested that he bring Deva home and raise her instead. Singaram's look filled her eyes with tears.

Could anger born against Deva leave Annam's home and enter Dhanapal's beautiful mansion? It fell upon George, especially while Singaram was away.

One rainy day the streets became mud. Would Singaram's street possess a tar road? Was he Dhanapal? George rolled in the mire, lost his white colour, and returned resembling the lowest street dog nosing at discarded leaves. Thinking of Deva, Annam felt a kind of satisfaction. If Deva came to live amid life's dirt, she too would lose her glitter and become like George. It was meaningless joy, but what more could Annam expect?

Singaram saw George's condition and also Annam grinning after allowing it. He raged, abused her, and raised a hand to strike, but stopped when he saw a crowd approaching.

The crowd accompanied Arumainathan, only son of Dhanapal's distant relative Kuzhandaivelu Mudaliar and president of the Social Service Association. Arumainathan had founded, conducted, and presided over that association himself. It received abundant government grants and donations from the wealthy.

Every newspaper praised him as one who had dedicated body, wealth, and soul to the welfare of the poor. No one counted whether even one illness of poverty had disappeared through his astonishing work. Nor should they, surely! Was Arumainathan himself a destitute pilgrim? He was a rich man's son.

He frequently inspected poor people's quarters: torn roofs, broken pots, collapsing walls, heaps of refuse, fly-infested places, and drains crawling with worms. He explained that such filth produced plague, influenza, and cholera, first killing the poor and then spreading through the city. He never discussed why the poor were compelled to remain amid that filth, and no one asked him to.

His eyes did not stop after observing dirt. He also closely inspected beautiful helpless women in dirty clothes. He tasted fruit that fell into his bowl; of the rest he said, “Shame, this fruit is sour.”

That day he came with Deva on Social Service Association business. A beautiful young woman and a lavishly dressed man arriving in a poor quarter naturally drew a crowd. Singaram forgot George and watched.

Deva's eyes poured anger upon him. “Is this how you raise a dog?”

Arumainathan asked for an explanation. Deva answered furiously: “Father did not want to give it. Mother too said it should not go to these daily-wage people. I alone insisted and obtained it for him, and he has reduced it to this condition.”

While Deva poured out anger, Singaram fearfully wiped the dirt from the puppy.

The word “daily-wage people” pierced his heart like a spear. Arumainathan's speech pierced it with a thousand.

“Deva, should such a fine puppy be given to these poor people? They themselves do not know how to live cleanly and roll in rubbish. We are engaged in reforming, washing, and bathing them. You are wrong to complain that he has not kept the puppy clean. George has dirt upon his body; look at this man's head, dry and unkempt; his shirt, so filthy; his feet, mud; his eyes, dust. You ignore his condition and attend to the dog. You are mad.”

“Yes,” Deva said, not because she accepted his reasoning, but because she had once imagined marrying such a man as Singaram.

Singaram could only weep. Annam muttered and snapped her fingers. What else could she do? The service-association president departed with the wealthy-eyed woman, but first looked once more at Annam's beauty.

Singaram understood his world. He regretted the vain desire to enjoy at least some features of wealthy life. Like the turkey that saw the forest peacock dance, he had created only useless turmoil.

Four or five days later, Arumainathan drove down that street, not for social service but hoping to see Annam. George lay unattended in the dust. The car wheel caught, crushed, and killed him. Arumainathan decided he could see Annam later and drove away.

“You sinner!” Annam screamed and gathered George into her arms. Singaram ran from inside at her cry. She pointed to the car. Silently he took George, shed two tears, dug a pit behind the house, and buried him. He buried not only George but his anger toward Annam, his love of display, and his old dreams.

Four or five years later, Annam sat “Karuppan” upon “Vellai” and watched them play. Believing he had been born by the grace of Karuppannasami, Singaram named his child Karuppannan. Vellai was their dog.

It ate neither biscuits nor drank milk. Somewhere, somehow, at some time it found food. It guarded only their house, served as a horse for the child, and endured blows from a stick. Yet it never forgot the household and wagged joyfully at every family member.

Once in a while Annam placed two handfuls of rice upon a discarded leaf and called, “Vellai! Vellai!” Vellai had been born in a ruined pavilion at the end of the street. Its mother and father were street scavengers, not pets bred by Dhanapal Chettiar. For one born in that “lineage,” this was a feast.

Singaram understood clearly that not only among humans but among every living creature, two kinds of lineage existed.

6 January 1965

---NOTES---
“பரம்பரை” can mean generation, descent, inherited line, or lineage. “Two Lineages” best preserves the ending's parallel between inherited human class and pedigree versus street-born dogs. Names Julia/Jimmy/George mark imported or elite pet culture; Vellai and Karuppannan are ordinary Tamil colour/deity names. The proverb comparing imitation to a turkey copying a forest peacock is retained by meaning. “அன்னாடிகள்” is a contemptuous class label for people living from day to day or by daily wages. The story's descriptions of poverty, domestic violence, class contempt, predatory charity, and disease belong to its social critique. Four scans omitted by OCR were manually transcribed before translation.
